Z||000042||Ken Wittlief||08-14-2001||02:07 PM||||thats the problem with the 220 outlet<P>sources 60 amps and has no GFI on it!<P>can you feel it brother? are you feeling it?<P>YEA!<P>[reminds me of a time I walked into a office space and two secretaries were trying to tell if the air conditioner was on or not - one had her hand way up in the air and was saying: "I can feel it - do you feel it? Im feeling it"<P>  I couldnt resist - I put my hand way up in the air, palm upwards, closed my eyes, and in my best Jimmy Swagart imatation said, "YEA! Im feeling it sisters! The POWER is there!"||||reg||1
Z||000043||JSocha||08-14-2001||03:27 PM||||  <IMG SRC="http://www.twinoaks4fun.com/images/beavis_hell.jpg">  <P>"Yeah, that was pretty cool. Huh-huh. It smelled cool, too. Huh-huh."<p>[This message has been edited by JSocha (edited 08-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000044||Songman||08-14-2001||04:10 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Ken Wittlief:<BR><B>In the end, performing music is an act of courage - having the guts to get up in front of people and in effect, say: "this is how I feel - this is the way I see things - this is what is important to me" in the hope that some of the people listening will identify and connect with what you are revealing about yourself through your music.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I do have to agree with this. It still scares me to death every time I walk out on a stage. Of course, Merle says after 40 years he still gets nervous too.<P>And I play real country music too. I won't play pop country just because that is what is being catered to 14 year old girls right now. I play what is in my heart. I couldn't play anything else and it be the real me.<P>Yes, when you hear a song you build an image. You think in your mind what the person might look like. But that image is usually skewed by how well you like the song and has nothing to do with the reality of the person. Tina said in another post that she was suprised because I didn't look like what she thinks of as a country singer. If she had heard my music first, she would probably have thought so even less.<P>But I've played Trampps in NYC, and places in just about every city in this country, and well as the rowdiest bars and VFWs this country has to offer, and no matter what the social standing, education, or bank account what mattered to them was the music. That's why they came. In all cases, if the music was good it made their lives better for just a little while.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000045||fogglethorpe||08-14-2001||04:55 PM||||CHEER UP!<P>At least you're not the one whose job it is to scrape the horrid sludgy bits from under the movie theater seats.<P>...hope that helps.||||reg||1
